<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Routines – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Routines</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Pick It Up!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple words, with teacher support (e.g., 'pen' or 'door'). Can engage in interactive activities to label objects related to classroom routines (e.g., write 'book' under the picture of a book). Can copy simple words with guidance (e.g., 'sit' and 'go').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Letter Sound & Action" warm-up. Students will review letter sounds (A, B, C, D, E) and corresponding actions. For 'P', students say /p/ and pretend to 'pick up' an object. For 'D', students say /d/ and pretend to move 'down'. The teacher shows flashcards with words like 'pen', 'paper', 'door', and students shout out the initial letter sound and then the word. This activates prior phonemic awareness and connects to the 'Pick It Up!' theme.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to trace the letter 'P' and 'D' on a large whiteboard or paper, emphasizing the correct stroke order. Then, the teacher models tracing the words 'pen' and 'door' below the letters, pronouncing them clearly. "Look, this is letter P. P for pen. I can trace 'pen'." The teacher demonstrates picking up a pen and saying, "Pick up the pen."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the traced word 'pen': "What word is this?" (Expected: "Pen!")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the traced word 'door': "Can you show me a door in our classroom?" (Students point.)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Routines")</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture cards of classroom objects (chair, desk, bag, pen, paper, notebook, door, eraser). Each card has the word clearly printed below the image. The teacher also uses a sequencing chart for a simple classroom routine: "Step 1: Sit. Step 2: Take pen. Step 3: Draw." Each step is illustrated with a picture and the imperative sentence written below.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student uses simple language to identify written words for common classroom objects and simple routine instructions. They develop phonemic awareness by connecting written letters to sounds /p/ and /d/ in context.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up a picture card of a 'chair' and asks: "What is this? This is a..." (Students complete: "chair!"). Teacher then points to the written word 'chair'. "This says 'chair'."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows the routine chart. For "Step 1: Sit", teacher asks: "What do we do in Step 1?" (Students say "Sit!"). Teacher traces the word 'Sit'.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows cards for 'pen' and 'paper' and asks, "What do we pick up?" to reinforce 'Pick it up' and object vocabulary.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice copying simple words related to classroom objects and routine actions (chair, desk, pen, paper, sit, pick up). They practice recognizing and writing initial letters (A, B, C, D, E) and simple imperatives ('Pick it up.').</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Tracing & Matching Words:</b> Students receive a worksheet with dotted outlines of capital letters A, B, C, D, E and simple words like 'pen', 'door', 'sit', 'go'. They trace the letters and words with pencils. Below, there are small pictures of a 'pen' and a 'door', and students draw a line from the traced word to its matching picture. Teacher circulates, providing individual support and checking tracing accuracy, emphasizing '/p/ in pick' and '/d/ in down' when words like 'pen' and 'door' are traced.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Interactive Labeling with Word Cards:</b> The teacher displays large pictures of classroom items (e.g., 'desk', 'chair', 'bag', 'notebook', 'paper') around the classroom. Students work in small groups. The teacher gives each group a set of word cards (e.g., 'desk', 'chair', 'bag', 'notebook', 'paper'). Students verbally identify the object, then match the correct word card to each picture. For example, they say "This is a desk," then place the 'desk' word card below the picture. The teacher then guides them to copy the word onto a sticky note and place it next to the word card.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Sequencing Routine Imperatives:</b> Students receive a worksheet with three blank boxes and a word bank: 'Sit', 'Take pen', 'Draw'. Below the boxes, there are small pictures representing "Step 1: Sit", "Step 2: Take pen", "Step 3: Draw". In pairs, students orally describe the routine ("First, sit!"). Then, they copy the words from the word bank into the correct box corresponding to the picture. For example, they copy 'Sit' into the first box, 'Take pen' into the second, and 'Draw' into the third. Teacher monitors, providing help with copying and checking for correct sequencing.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Routines")</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Classroom Labeling Project.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students will work in small groups of 3-4 to create labels for real classroom objects, linking to the theme "Pick It Up!" and "Our Routines."</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives a set of pre-cut paper strips and a marker.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher assigns specific objects to each group (e.g., Group 1: 'chair', 'desk'; Group 2: 'door', 'bag'; Group 3: 'pen', 'paper').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">With the teacher's direct assistance and a word bank displayed on the board (e.g., CHAIR, DESK, DOOR, BAG, PEN, PAPER, NOTEBOOK, ERASER, SIT, PICK UP), students collaboratively copy the assigned words onto their paper strips to create labels. The teacher explicitly reminds them to copy carefully.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once labels are written, groups take turns affixing their labels to the actual objects in the classroom. As they label, they say, "This is a [chair]," or "This is the [door]."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, the teacher introduces a "Clean Up Routine" chart with pictures and simple written imperatives: "Step 1: Pick up paper. Step 2: Put pens in bag. Step 3: Sit down." Students, with teacher guidance, try to trace or copy the key words like 'Pick up' and 'paper' from the chart onto small cards to create their own 'routine labels'. They use the interjection 'Please' when prompted: "Please pick up the paper."</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in copying words, collaborative identification and labeling, and associating written words with real-world objects and routine actions. Emphasis on using target vocabulary: 'chair', 'desk', 'bag', 'pen', 'paper', 'door', 'pick up', 'sit', 'down', 'Please'.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and a simple "Label My Classroom" worksheet.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete an individual "Label My Classroom" worksheet. The worksheet features simple line drawings of the classroom objects learned (chair, desk, pen, paper, door, bag). Below each picture, there is a blank line for the student to copy the correct word from a provided word bank at the top of the page. The word bank includes all target vocabulary words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation and Worksheet):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Legibility:</b> Can the student's copied words be recognized (recognizing letter shapes and sequence)?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Accuracy of Labeling:</b> Does the student copy the correct word for each picture? (e.g., 'pen' under the picture of a pen).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Participation:</b> Actively participates in group labeling and routine activities (observed during Production).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Attempts to copy/write target words (e.g., 'pen', 'door', 'sit', 'pick up').</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw a happy face, neutral face, or sad face next to each statement on a small paper slip:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write 'pen'."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can label a 'door'."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can copy words for our routine."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new word can you write today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is your favorite classroom object to label?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What activities worked best for student engagement and writing output? Were the scaffolding and word banks sufficient for all students to copy words? Were the letters and words sufficiently large and clear for tracing and copying? What adjustments are needed for students who struggled with letter formation or matching words to pictures? I observed good engagement in the collaborative labeling. I need to reinforce the specific phonemic sounds /p/ and /d/ more clearly in relation to the written words for some students.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their knowledge of labeled classroom objects and routine commands to create a "classroom rules" poster (21st Century Skills Project) incorporating simple pictures and copied words (e.g., "Please sit," "Pick up paper").</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
</table>